ntinel surveillance data in a maturing epidemic: comparative analysis of HIV prevalence in sentinel surveillance and Uganda HIV/AIDS Sero-behavioral survey data 2004&#x02013;05. 2007 (draft manuscript).</article-title></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b18"><label>18</label><mixed-citation publication-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Montana</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Spencer</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group> <source>Incorporating geographic information into MEASURE surveys: a field guide to GPS data collection</source>. <publisher-loc>Calverton, MD</publisher-loc>: <publisher-name>Macro International</publisher-name>, <year>2004</year></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b19"><label>19</label><mixed-citation publication-type="book"><collab>WHO/UNAIDS/CDC</collab> <source>Guidelines for conducting HIV sentinel serosurveys among pregnant women and other groups</source>. <publisher-loc>Geneva</publisher-loc>: <publisher-name>WHO/UNAIDS/CDC</publisher-name>, <year>2003</year></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b20"><label>20</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><collab>WHO/HealthMapper Uganda (version 4</collab><article-title>2).</article-title> <comment>Geneva: WHO</comment>, <year>2006</year></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b21"><label>21</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><collab>KEMRI/Wellcome Trust Collaborative Programme, TALA Research Group, Department of Zoology, University of Oxford, Ministry of Health and Centre for Tropical Medicine; International Livestock Research Institute: Nairobi, Kenya, 2007</collab></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b22"><label>22</label><mixed-citation publication-type="book"><collab>World Health Organization</collab> <source>WHO/service availability mapping: Kenya</source>. <publisher-loc>Geneva</publisher-loc>: <publisher-name>WHO</publisher-name>, <year>2005</year></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b23"><label>23</label><mixed-cit